-          "claim": "A stepper attempts a finite number $S$ of steps. By [F3], each attempted step makes at most one Newton call; on a root status it reevaluates the block residual once to record the unscaled blocks, and otherwise returns immediately. Summing at most $B+2$ evaluations per attempted step gives $S(B+2)$ residual evaluations; after the last accepted step it returns completed.",
+          "claim": "A backward-Euler call first evaluates its starting algebraic residual once, and a BDF2 call evaluates its two starting algebraic residuals, by [F3] and [F4]. Thus their preliminary checks use at most one and two evaluations, respectively, even when a bound fails. Each attempted step makes at most one Newton call; on a root status the wrapper reevaluates the block residual once to record the unscaled blocks, and otherwise returns immediately. Each attempted step therefore adds at most $B+2$ evaluations. Summing gives $1+S(B+2)$ for backward Euler and $2+S(B+2)$ for BDF2. The pre-call membership tests add no residual evaluation, and all loops and tests are finite.",
